# Append-only files: take both sides on merge instead of conflicting.
#
# Every feature branch adds a bullet under `## Unreleased`, always at the same
# offset, so N parallel PRs produce N-1 conflicts — each one resolved the same
# way (keep both) and each one costing a push and a full CI cycle. The v0.9.5
# cycle had six parallel branches and burned five rounds of this.
#
# `union` is a built-in git merge driver: on conflict it keeps both sides in
# order rather than writing markers. That is exactly right for an append-only
# log and exactly wrong for anything where the two sides might be alternatives,
# so keep this list narrow — one entry, deliberately.
#
# SCOPE — read this before assuming it fixed the problem above. `union` applies
# only where GIT does the merge. GitHub's server-side merge does NOT apply
# .gitattributes merge drivers, so a second CHANGELOG-touching PR still reports
# "Pull Request has merge conflicts" and its merge button still fails. Since
# this repo lands work via PR, that is the common path — meaning this driver
# does NOT eliminate the N-1 conflicts described above. Verified on a real
# CHANGELOG-touching merge: GitHub refused it; the identical merge run locally
# reported "Automatic merge went well" with zero conflicted files.
#
# What it does buy: the resolution becomes mechanical instead of manual —
#   git fetch origin && git merge origin/main   # union auto-resolves
#   git push                                    # GitHub re-computes: mergeable
# No markers, no hand-editing. Cost per conflict drops from "edit by hand" to
# "one local merge", not to zero.
#
# This does NOT remove the need to look: union resolves silently, so a bad merge
# leaves no trace. It can still put a bullet under the wrong heading if a branch
# introduces its own `### Added`/`### Fixed`. The standard headings are
# pre-seeded under `## Unreleased` so branches add bullets under existing
# headings instead of creating duplicates. Leave them in place when you cut a
# release.
#
# That check is no longer a matter of remembering. `tests/test_changelog_structure.py`
# asserts exactly one of each seeded heading under `## Unreleased` and fails CI on a
# duplicate, a missing heading, an invented one, or a bullet above the first heading.
# It exists because the silent case actually happened: merging main into a
# release branch produced two `### Fixed` headings with no conflict marker and
# nothing in `git status` — caught by hand, one merge before a release.
docs/CHANGELOG.md merge=union
